nancy (including colorectal adenocarcinoma), a new gingival 'granuloma-like' mass should prompt biopsy to exclude metastasis.\"\n    ],\n    \"histopathology_and_IHC_for_colorectal_origin\": [\n      \"Histology of metastatic colorectal adenocarcinoma in oral soft tissue typically shows infiltrative malignant glands/tubules in the lamina propria/submucosa; mucin production may be present. 'Dirty necrosis' (necrotic debris within glandular lumina) is a classic supportive feature of colorectal origin (not always present).\",\n      \"Immunohistochemistry profile supportive of colorectal primary: CDX2 positive (intestinal differentiation), CK20 positive, SATB2 positive; often CK7 negative. This CK20+/CDX2+ (±SATB2+)/CK7− pattern helps distinguish colorectal metastasis from many salivary/upper aerodigestive primaries and from most primary oral squamous cell carcinomas.\",\n      \"Primary oral squamous cell carcinoma would show squamous differentiation (keratinization/intercellular bridges) and squamous markers (e.g., p40/p63) rather than gland-forming adenocarcinoma with intestinal markers.\"\n    ],\n    \"distinguishing_irritation_fibroma_vs_pyogenic_granuloma_like_metastasis\": [\n      \"Irritation fibroma (traumatic fibroma): benign reactive hyperplasia from chronic irritation; usually small (<1–1.5 cm), slow-growing, firm/rubbery, smooth surface, mucosa-colored or slightly paler; typically not very friable and does not bleed easily unless secondarily traumatized. Ulceration is uncommon unless repeatedly bitten/traumatized.\",\n      \"Metastasis mimicking pyogenic granuloma: tends to be larger, more vascular-appearing (red-purple), friable, often ulcerated, and bleeds readily with minimal trauma (e.g., brushing). Often shows rapid growth and may occur in older patients, especially with a known cancer history.\",\n      \"Key practical discriminator: size/rapidity/friability + cancer history favor metastasis; definitive distinction requires biopsy because clinical